@meta
id: 13-19
title: Stop the match early
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-zs-ze
par: 40
minutes: 6
vscode: rename three keys by hand, checking each value first
open: worker.config.ts

@brief
The config keys that hold a timeout in milliseconds are being renamed to
say so: `captureTimeout` becomes `captureTimeoutMs`, and the same for
the other two whose value is a bare number. `heartbeatTimeout` holds an
ISO duration string and keeps its name. `timeoutUnit` and
`timeoutRetries` are not timeouts at all.

So the rule is: a key ending in `Timeout`, *whose value is a number*.
The value is how you tell them apart, but the value is not what you
edit — you are appending to the key. The pattern has to look past the
end of the thing it lands on.

`\zs` moved the start of the match forward. Its twin moves the end
back.

@keys
| `\ze` | "end the match here" — everything after it is checked but not matched |
| `/\vTimeout\ze: \d+` | `Timeout`, but only when a number follows — and the match is just `Timeout` |
| `/pattern/e` | land on the last character of the match — which `\ze` now controls |
| `a` | append after the cursor |
| `n.` | next match, same offset, same append |

@hints
The value has to be in the pattern so the string-valued key is skipped.
But if the value is part of the match, `/e` lands you on the value.
--
`\ze` says "the match stops here, but keep checking". Put it between
`Timeout` and the `: ` that follows, and the `/e` offset lands on the
`t` of `Timeout`.
--
`/\vTimeout\ze: \d+,$/e` `Enter`, `aMs`, `Esc`, then `n.` twice.

@solution
/\vTimeout\ze: \d+,$/e<Enter>  aMs<Esc>  n.  n.  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-five keystrokes. Each key was checked against its value and
edited without touching it.

`\ze` — "zero-width end" — is the mirror of `\zs`. Everything after it
must be there for the pattern to match, but it is outside the match. So
`Timeout\ze: \d+` only matches a `Timeout` that is followed by a
colon, a space and digits, and yet the match is the seven letters of
`Timeout` and nothing more. The `/e` offset put you on the `t`; `gn`
would have selected just the word; `hlsearch` highlights just the word.

Other regex engines call this a lookahead and spell it `(?=...)`. Vim's
version is simpler to type and, with `\zs`, symmetrical: `\zs` is
"after this", `\ze` is "before this", and together they say "the bit in
the middle of this". The next lesson is a drill on `\zs`; the scenarios
at the end of the chapter use both.

There was a way to do this one without `\ze` — land on the `T` and
press `e` — and it is fine. The point is not that `\ze` was
indispensable here. It is that "match this, but only when followed by
that" is a thing you can now say, and a pattern that can say it lands
on fewer wrong things.

@start worker.config.ts
import { QUEUE_PREFIX } from './queue.constants';

export const workerConfig = {
  queue: `${QUEUE_PREFIX}.refunds.v2`,
  prefetch: 10,
  captureTimeout: 30000,
  timeoutUnit: 'ms',
  refundTimeout: 5000,
  heartbeatTimeout: 'PT30S',
  webhookTimeout: 8000,
  timeoutRetries: 3,
  deadLetter: `${QUEUE_PREFIX}.refunds.v2.dead`,
};

@expected worker.config.ts
import { QUEUE_PREFIX } from './queue.constants';

export const workerConfig = {
  queue: `${QUEUE_PREFIX}.refunds.v2`,
  prefetch: 10,
  captureTimeoutMs: 30000,
  timeoutUnit: 'ms',
  refundTimeoutMs: 5000,
  heartbeatTimeout: 'PT30S',
  webhookTimeoutMs: 8000,
  timeoutRetries: 3,
  deadLetter: `${QUEUE_PREFIX}.refunds.v2.dead`,
};
